SPECS.basement = { name:"Basement Conversion", region:"England",
 cats:["Planning & Party Wall","Applicability","General Notes","Ground Investigation & Water","Structure & Underpinning","Waterproofing (BS 8102)","Basement Walls","External Walls Above Ground","Party Wall","Additional Notes for Walls","Basement Floors","Floor Over Basement","Internal Works","Lightwells & External Works","Staircases & Headroom","Means of Escape & Fire Regs","Ventilation","Condensation (C2)","Drainage & Pumping","Services","Conservation of Fuel & Power"],
 buildups:[
  {g:"BW", c:"Basement Walls", t:"New Reinforced Concrete Retaining Wall — Type A + Type C Waterproofing, Insulated Lining", u:"0.13 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K for a new wall, and Grade 3 (habitable) waterproofing to BS 8102:2022",
   p:[
    "New basement wall of 300mm minimum reinforced concrete to the structural engineer's design, cast against the underpinning or against a temporary works face, in a concrete of the designated strength and sulfate class from BS 8500 for the ground conditions with a water/cement ratio not exceeding 0.45 and crack control reinforcement to limit crack widths to 0.2mm, with hydrophilic waterstops at every kicker and construction joint.",
    "Waterproofing as a combined system for Grade 3 to BS 8102:2022: a Type A barrier protection of a bonded bentonite or bituminous sheet membrane, or a cementitious multi-coat system, to the external face or the internal face as the specialist design, and a Type C cavity drainage system internally of a studded high-density polyethylene membrane fixed with sealed plugs, drained at the base into a perimeter channel with rodding access at each change of direction, discharging to a sump with duplicate pumps, battery back-up and a high-level alarm. Two forms of protection are the recommendation of BS 8102 for habitable basements where the consequence of leakage is unacceptable.",
    "Internal lining of 100mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard or K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) fixed to treated battens over the cavity drain membrane, or independent metal studs clear of the membrane with the board between, a continuous vapour control layer taped at all joints and to the floor and ceiling, and 12.5mm plasterboard skimmed; no fixing is to penetrate the membrane other than the manufacturer's sealed plugs.",
    "The below-ground wall calculates at 0.13 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a basement 2.7m below ground with the floor as BF1, allowing for the ground; the same wall above ground would calculate at 0.18 and the portion of any wall exposed above ground is to be treated as an external wall."]},
  {g:"BW", c:"Basement Walls", t:"Existing Masonry Basement Wall Retained — Type C Cavity Drainage, Insulated Stud Lining", u:"0.14 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.30 W/m²K for a retained wall becoming part of the thermal envelope, and Grade 3 waterproofing to BS 8102:2022",
   p:[
    "Where an existing cellar wall of brick or stone is retained, remove any existing render or bitumen paint, rake out and repoint open joints with a lime or cement mortar to suit the masonry, and make good any voids so the wall is stable and continuous behind the membrane. Where the wall is below the water table or shows active water ingress, the wall is first to be sealed with a cementitious tanking slurry to slow the flow, and the engineer is to confirm the wall can take the hydrostatic load.",
    "Type C cavity drainage system of a studded high-density polyethylene membrane fixed to the wall with sealed plugs at the manufacturer's centres, all joints sealed with the proprietary tape, carried down into a perimeter drainage channel at the wall and floor junction with rodding and jetting access at every corner, and connected to a sump as BF1. Where the existing wall is a party wall, the membrane and channel run continuously along it and the neighbouring owner's cellar is not to be affected.",
    "Insulated lining of independent 70mm metal studs set 25mm clear of the membrane with 70mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) between and a 37.5mm insulated plasterboard lining with a vapour control layer, joints taped, or a timber stud lining of the same depth on a damp proof course. This build-up calculates at 0.14 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 as a below-ground wall for a 2.7m deep basement; 100mm of the framing board calculates at 0.12.",
    "NOTE — A cavity drainage system depends on the drainage channel and pumps being maintained. The membrane manufacturer's maintenance schedule, the rodding point locations and the pump service interval are to be handed to the client and recorded in the operation and maintenance information. A basement that is sold on with the sump inaccessible is a basement that will flood."]},
  {g:"BW", c:"Basement Walls", t:"New Waterproof Concrete Retaining Wall — Type B + Type C, Insulated Lining", u:"0.13 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K for a new wall, and Grade 3 (habitable) waterproofing to BS 8102:2022",
   p:[
    "Where the specialist design adopts structurally integral protection, the basement walls and slab are to be a watertight reinforced concrete structure to BS EN 1992-3 designed for tightness class 1 (Type B protection): a concrete of the designated strength and sulfate class from BS 8500 with a water/cement ratio not exceeding 0.45, a waterproofing admixture where the specialist specifies one, crack control reinforcement to limit crack widths to 0.2mm, hydrophilic strips and injectable hoses at every construction joint and kicker, the wall and slab kicker cast together where the sequence allows, and every penetration formed with a puddle flange or a resin-sealed sleeve. The concrete is to be placed and cured to the specialist's method statement with no joints other than those detailed, and inspected before each pour.",
    "Type B protection is combined with a Type C cavity drainage system internally as the second form of protection for Grade 3, with the perimeter channel, sump and duplicate pumps as build-up BW1, and the insulated lining of 100mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 or K112 board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) on battens or independent studs clear of the membrane with a vapour control layer and 12.5mm plasterboard. The below-ground wall calculates at 0.13 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a basement 2.7m below ground with the floor as BF1.",
    "NOTE — Waterproof concrete is only as good as its joints and its pouring. It suits a new-dig basement with a clear sequence and a specialist contractor; it does not suit an underpinned conversion where the wall is cast in 1m bays against the existing footing, and Type A plus Type C is specified for those."]},
  {g:"BW", c:"Basement Walls", t:"Basement Wall — Cavity Drain Membrane with Block Inner Leaf", u:"0.14 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K for a new wall or 0.30 for a retained wall, and Grade 3 waterproofing to BS 8102:2022",
   p:[
    "Where a masonry inner leaf is preferred to a stud lining, for robustness or to carry fixings, provide the Type C cavity drainage membrane to the new or existing retaining wall as BW1 or BW2, drained to the perimeter channel, and build a 100mm aircrete inner leaf of 0.11 W/mK off a thickening to the slab or the slab kicker, clear of the membrane with a 75mm cavity fully filled with 75mm Kingspan Kooltherm K108 Cavity Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK), tied to the retaining wall with stainless steel ties drilled and resin fixed through the membrane using the membrane manufacturer's sealed plugs only, and finished with 12.5mm plasterboard on dabs; the base of the leaf on a damp proof course above the channel so the channel remains accessible for rodding.",
    "With 50mm of board this build-up calculates at 0.18 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 as a below-ground wall for a basement 2.7m below ground, meeting the new-wall standard with no margin; the 75mm board specified calculates at 0.14 and a 100mm cavity with 100mm board at 0.12. The inner leaf costs 200mm of room width on each wall and adds load to the slab, which the engineer is to allow for.",
    "NOTE — Every tie that penetrates the cavity drain membrane is a potential leak and the plugs are to be the manufacturer's sealed type installed with the sealing rope; a stud lining fixed only to the floor and ceiling avoids penetrating the membrane at all and is the preferred lining where the wall is not to carry heavy fixings."]},
  {g:"BF", c:"Basement Floors", t:"New Reinforced Concrete Basement Slab — Type A + Type C Waterproofing, Insulation Over", u:"0.13 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K for a new floor, and Grade 3 waterproofing to BS 8102:2022",
   p:[
    "New basement slab of 200mm minimum reinforced concrete to the structural engineer's design, designed for the hydrostatic uplift and the ground bearing pressure, on 50mm blinding over 150mm compacted clean stone with a perimeter land drain where the specialist requires it, with the same concrete specification, waterstops and crack control as the walls, and the slab and wall kicker cast together where the sequence allows.",
    "Waterproofing as BW1: Type A barrier protection beneath or over the slab as the specialist design, and a Type C cavity drainage floor membrane of 8mm stud height with all joints taped, laid to fall to the perimeter channel and to a sump chamber of 500mm x 500mm x 700mm minimum set into the slab, fitted with duplicate submersible pumps each capable of the design flow, a battery back-up unit giving not less than four hours of operation, a high-level alarm sounding within the dwelling, and a sealed lid with the discharge rising to a trapped connection into the surface water drain, or into a combined sewer with the sewerage undertaker's consent, with a non-return valve; groundwater is not to be discharged into a separate foul drain.",
    "Floor build-up over the membrane of 100mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) with 25mm perimeter upstands, a 500 gauge separating layer and a 75mm sand and cement screed reinforced with fibres or D49 mesh, with underfloor heating pipes clipped to the insulation where specified. The basement floor calculates at 0.13 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a basement 2.7m below ground with a perimeter/area ratio of 0.6 on clay.",
    "NOTE — The sump and pumps are the single most important component of a cavity drained basement. They are to be sized by the waterproofing specialist for the measured or estimated inflow, wired to a dedicated circuit, tested at commissioning by filling the sump, and their location shown on the drawings so the client can reach them."]},
  {g:"BF", c:"Basement Floors", t:"Existing Cellar Floor Lowered — New Slab with Insulation Over", u:"0.15 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K for a new floor, and Grade 3 waterproofing to BS 8102:2022",
   p:[
    "Where the existing cellar floor is lowered to gain headroom, the existing floor is to be broken out and the ground reduced only after the walls have been underpinned or the engineer has confirmed the founding level of the existing footings is below the new formation; excavation below the footing of an unprotected wall is not permitted. New slab of 150mm minimum reinforced concrete to the engineer's design on a damp proof membrane and 150mm compacted stone with 50mm blinding, cast with a kicker at the walls to receive the wall membrane.",
    "Type C cavity drainage floor membrane with perimeter channel and sump as BF1, 80mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) with perimeter upstands, a separating layer and a 65mm screed. Where the floor to ceiling height is critical, 60mm of the same board with a 50mm proprietary screed calculates at 0.18 and meets the new-floor standard with no margin. The 80mm build-up calculates at 0.15 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a 2.7m deep basement with a perimeter/area ratio of 0.6 on clay."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls Above Ground", t:"Existing External Wall Above Ground — Internally Insulated", u:"0.26 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.30 W/m²K for a retained wall becoming part of the thermal envelope (0.18 where the wall is new)",
   p:[
    "Where the basement wall rises above external ground level, or the conversion brings a semi-basement room with a part-exposed wall into the heated envelope, the above-ground portion is an external wall and is to be insulated internally: 72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard on dabs or battens to a 215mm solid brick wall with a continuous ribbon of adhesive at every perimeter and opening and the joints taped, which calculates at 0.26 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946; to an uninsulated cavity wall 62.5mm calculates at 0.29. The external face is to be made weathertight and any damp proof course reinstated before the lining is fixed, and the lining is to lap the below-ground membrane so there is no cold strip at the junction.",
    "Where the above-ground wall is new, it is to be built to the new-wall standard of 0.18 W/m²K as the House Extension specification cavity wall build-ups, with the damp proof course at 150mm above ground linked to the basement waterproofing as the Above-Ground Portions note."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"Party Wall", t:"Existing Party Wall in the Basement", u:"0.26 W/m²K / REI 60",
   tgt:"To maintain the separating wall to the adjoining building as a compartment wall with 60 minutes fire resistance, and to insulate it where the neighbour's cellar is unheated",
   p:[
    "The party wall in the basement separates two buildings and is to be made good in masonry wherever there are gaps, open perpends, redundant flues or timber built in, carried up and fire stopped at every floor, and not chased for sockets back to back, so it gives 60 minutes fire resistance; where it is to be underpinned, the underpinning is to the party wall award and the engineer's sequence. The wall is also part of the waterproofing envelope: the Type C cavity drainage membrane and its channel run continuously along it as the basement walls, fixed with the manufacturer's sealed plugs, and the neighbour's cellar is not to be affected by the drainage.",
    "Where the neighbour's cellar or basement is unheated the wall is a thermal element to an unheated space and is to be insulated to 0.30 W/m²K: an insulated stud lining as BW2 achieves this, or 72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard on battens over the membrane calculates at 0.26 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 with both faces treated as internal surfaces; where the neighbour's basement is already a heated room no insulation is needed but the fire and waterproofing treatment still applies. Work to the party wall is notifiable under the Party Wall etc. Act 1996 as the Planning & Party Wall note."]},
  {g:"IW", c:"Internal Works", t:"Internal Partition in the Basement", u:"40 dB Rw",
   tgt:"To meet Requirement E2 between a bedroom or a room containing a WC and other rooms, and 30 minutes fire resistance where the partition encloses the basement stair",
   p:[
    "Internal partitions in the basement of 70mm metal C studs or 89mm x 38mm treated timber studs at 600mm centres with the base track or sole plate on a damp proof course above the cavity drain floor membrane and fixed to the screed only, never through the membrane, 50mm minimum mineral wool infill and 12.5mm plasterboard each side with joints taped and perimeters sealed. Where the partition forms the enclosure to a protected stair from the basement it is to give 30 minutes fire resistance with two layers of 12.5mm plasterboard or one of 15mm fire-resisting board on the room side and FD30S doorsets as the Internal Works note. Masonry partitions of 100mm blockwork may be used where the slab is designed for them and the base is kept clear of the perimeter channel."]},
  {g:"IF", c:"Floor Over Basement", t:"Existing Timber Floor Over Basement — Upgraded for Fire and Sound", u:"REI 30",
   tgt:"To achieve 30 minutes fire resistance to the floor over the basement and reasonable sound insulation between the basement and the ground floor",
   p:[
    "Existing timber ground floor over the basement to be retained where the joists are sound and adequate to the structural engineer's inspection, with any joist affected by rot or beetle cut back and spliced or replaced and all timber bearing on the basement walls isolated from the masonry by a damp proof course or joist hangers. New ceiling to the basement of two layers of 12.5mm plasterboard, or one layer of 15mm Type F fire-resisting board, with joints staggered and taped, fixed to resilient bars across the joists where sound insulation between the basement and the room above is wanted, and 100mm mineral wool of not less than 10 kg/m³ laid between the joists.",
    "Downlighters are not to be installed in the basement ceiling unless they are fire-rated fittings tested in the ceiling construction, and services passing through the ceiling are to be fire stopped. Where the floor over the basement is concrete, a 100mm concrete slab gives the fire resistance without a ceiling.",
    "NOTE — A basement with a habitable room is reached by a stair that may be the only way out, and the floor above it is the barrier between a basement fire and the sleeping accommodation above. The 30 minute ceiling is not optional and is to be inspected before it is decorated."]}
 ],
 notes:[
  {c:"Planning & Party Wall", t:"Planning Status", p:[
    "Conversion of an existing cellar into habitable accommodation without external alteration is normally permitted development. Excavation of a new basement, the enlargement of an existing cellar, and the formation of a lightwell, external stair or window to the front elevation normally require planning permission, and many London boroughs have adopted basement policies requiring a Basement Impact Assessment covering ground stability, groundwater and drainage, a construction method statement and limits on the depth and extent of the excavation below the garden. Where the property is listed or in a conservation area, separate consent is required and the works may be resisted.",
    "Building Regulations approval does not confer planning permission, and planning permission does not confer Building Regulations approval."]},
  {c:"Planning & Party Wall", t:"Party Wall etc. Act 1996", p:[
    "Excavation within 3m of a neighbouring building to a depth below the underside of its foundations, or within 6m where the excavation intersects a line drawn at 45 degrees down from the underside of the neighbour's foundation, is notifiable under Section 6 of the Party Wall etc. Act 1996, and underpinning, cutting into or raising a party wall is notifiable under Section 2. Notices are to be served by the building owner not less than one month (Section 6) or two months (Section 2) before the work starts, with the engineer's drawings and method statement, and an award made where the adjoining owner dissents. The award will normally require a condition survey, monitoring of the neighbouring property during the works, and working hours. No excavation or underpinning is to start until the Act has been complied with.",
    "NOTE — Basement works are the most common source of party wall disputes and of damage claims. The engineer's method statement, sequence and monitoring proposals are to be settled before the notices are served, not after."]},
  {c:"Applicability", t:"How the Regulations Apply", p:[
    "Conversion of a cellar or basement to habitable use is a material alteration and, because the basement walls and floor become part of the thermal envelope, a change in energy status of those elements under Approved Document L Volume 1 Section 4. The new or upgraded elements are to achieve the standards for new elements (walls and floors 0.18 W/m²K) where they are newly constructed and for retained elements (walls 0.30, floors 0.25 W/m²K) where existing construction is retained, subject to the technical feasibility caveat in the Approved Document. Where the basement is excavated as new construction beneath the house or as a new extension below ground, it is an extension to which the extension provisions apply, including the limit on the area of openings and the option of a whole-dwelling SAP calculation.",
    "The formation of a habitable room in a basement engages Part B for escape, Part C for resistance to moisture and ground gas, Part F for ventilation, Part K for the stair and headroom, and Part P for the electrical work. Where the basement becomes a self-contained flat it is a material change of use under Regulation 5 and the Flat Conversion specification applies in addition."]},
  {c:"Applicability", t:"Radon and Ground Gas", p:[
    "A basement habitable room is the most exposed room in the house to radon and ground gas because it is below ground on every side. Where the property is in a radon affected area on the UK radon map, protection to BR 211 is to be built in: basic protection by a radon-resistant membrane of a certificated grade across the floor and up the walls, sealed at every lap and penetration and continuous with the wall waterproofing, and full protection, where the map shows the higher probability band, by adding a radon sump beneath the slab with a capped pipe to the outside for a fan and, for a cavity drained basement, arranging the drainage void so it can be depressurised or vented. Where the site is on fill or near a former landfill or industrial use, gas protection to BS 8485 is to be designed from the site investigation. The radon status of the postcode is to be checked at design stage and recorded on the drawings, and a radon test carried out after occupation where protection has been installed."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Regulation 16 Notices, Inspections and Completion Documents", p:[
    "Regulation 16 of the Building Regulations 2010 (as amended in October 2023) requires three notices to the building control body: notice of intention to start work, given so that at least two clear days elapse before work begins; a commencement notice within five days of the work being regarded as commenced under Regulation 46A, which for a new building or a horizontal extension is when the sub-structure including the ground floor structure is complete and for other work (loft, garage, basement and flat conversions) is when 15 per cent of the work is complete, after which the authority has four weeks to reject the notice; and a completion notice within five days of completion. The completion notice must give the name, address, telephone number and email of the client, the principal contractor and the principal designer, state that the work is complete, include a signed statement by the client that the work complies with the Building Regulations, and include signed statements from the principal designer and principal contractor confirming that they have fulfilled their duties under Part 2A with the dates of their appointments; where a statement cannot be obtained the reason is to be given.",
    "Inspection stages to be notified to the Building Control Officer as they arise and as the building control body's inspection schedule sets out: the excavation of each underpinning bay before it is concreted, new foundations before concreting, the waterproofing before it is covered, the slab and wall reinforcement before each pour, damp proof courses and membranes, structural steel and the fire-resisting construction before it is closed up, insulation before it is boarded, the drainage and pumping installation before it is covered and on test, and completion. Certificates from competent person schemes (electrical, gas, replacement windows, heating appliances, unvented hot water) and commissioning notices for fixed building services and ventilation are to be given to building control within the periods the Regulations set (normally five days) and copied to the client with the completion certificate, together with the waterproofing design statement and guarantees, the pump commissioning record and the maintenance schedule, which purchasers' solicitors will ask for.",
    "NOTE — The two-day and five-day limbs are separate duties. Missing the commencement notice means the work is not treated as commenced for transitional purposes, which matters where an edition of the Approved Documents is about to change."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Compliance and Scope", p:[
    "All work to be carried out in accordance with the Building Regulations 2010 as amended, the current Approved Documents and the British Standards referred to in them, in particular BS 8102:2022 for waterproofing, BS 8004 for foundations and BS EN 1997 for geotechnical design, and to the drawings, the structural engineer's details and the waterproofing specialist's design. Where there is a conflict the drawings take precedence for dimensions, the engineer's information for structure and the specialist's design for waterproofing. Materials to be fit for their purpose and installed in accordance with the manufacturer's instructions and any third-party certificate.",
    "The waterproofing design is to be prepared by a specialist holding the Certificated Surveyor in Structural Waterproofing qualification or equivalent, who is to be appointed at design stage as BS 8102 recommends and is to inspect the installation. The contractor is to be experienced in basement construction and is to provide a method statement and sequence for the excavation, underpinning and temporary works approved by the engineer before work starts."]},
  {c:"Ground Investigation & Water", t:"Ground Investigation and Groundwater", p:[
    "A ground investigation to BS 5930 and BS EN 1997-2 is to be carried out before the design is finalised, with boreholes or trial pits to below the proposed formation level to establish the strata, the founding level and condition of the existing footings, the groundwater level and its seasonal variation, and the sulfate and chemical class of the ground and groundwater. Standpipes are to be monitored over a period long enough to establish the highest water level, and the waterproofing grade, the structural design for hydrostatic pressure and the drainage design are to be based on the highest credible water table, not the level found on the day of the investigation.",
    "Where the site is on the London Clay the design is to allow for heave of the clay beneath the new slab following excavation, either by a suspended or a heave-protected slab to the engineer's design; where the site is on the river terrace gravels or over a perched water table the inflow during construction is to be controlled by dewatering with the consent of the Environment Agency where an abstraction licence is needed. Sites with a history of flooding, or within a flood zone or a critical drainage area, are to be checked against the Environment Agency flood map and the borough strategic flood risk assessment, and sleeping accommodation is not to be provided in a basement in Flood Zone 3."]},
  {c:"Structure & Underpinning", t:"Structural Design and Temporary Works", p:[
    "All structural work to the design and details of the structural engineer, including the underpinning, the retaining walls designed for the earth and hydrostatic pressures and surcharge from adjoining buildings and the highway, the basement slab designed for uplift, the transfer of the existing loads through the new structure, and the temporary works at every stage. The design is to be to BS EN 1997 and BS EN 1992 with the ground parameters from the investigation, and the calculations submitted to building control before work begins. Movement monitoring of the existing building and the neighbouring properties is to be installed before excavation and read at the intervals the engineer and the party wall award specify, with trigger levels and actions defined.",
    "Temporary works to the design of a temporary works engineer and coordinated by a temporary works coordinator, with no excavation left open and unsupported, and the sequence on site not to depart from the approved method statement without the engineer's written agreement."]},
  {c:"Structure & Underpinning", t:"Lintels, Beams and Fire Protection", p:[
    "New openings in the basement walls for the lightwell window, the stair or an opening to an adjoining room are to have proprietary steel or precast concrete lintels sized for the span and the retained load with not less than 150mm end bearing, or a reinforced concrete downstand cast with the wall to the engineer's design; steel beams supporting the floor over the basement to BS EN 10025 on padstones, primed and protected to the same period of fire resistance as the floor over the basement (30 minutes, as the Means of Escape & Fire Regs note) by encasement in fire-resisting board or an intumescent coating to the certified thickness, with the certificate given to building control. Steelwork in a basement is to be protected against corrosion to a specification for a damp environment, with any steel behind the cavity drain membrane galvanised."]},
  {c:"Structure & Underpinning", t:"New Foundations", p:[
    "New foundations for lightwell walls, external stairs, a basement extension beyond the house footprint or a new internal wall are to be strip or trench-fill foundations to the engineer's design taken to the same depth as the underpinning where they adjoin it, in the sulfate class of concrete for the ground, with pipes passing through them sleeved or bridged with 50mm clearance and the excavations inspected before concrete is placed. Where a new foundation is within 3m of the neighbour's building and deeper than theirs the Party Wall etc. Act Section 6 notice applies."]},
  {c:"Structure & Underpinning", t:"Underpinning", p:[
    "Underpinning of the existing walls in mass concrete or reinforced concrete to the engineer's design, in a hit-and-miss sequence of bays not exceeding 1m in length (1.2m where the engineer permits) in the order shown on the engineer's drawings, typically 1-3-5 then 2-4, with no two adjacent bays open at the same time and no bay left open overnight. Each bay to be excavated to the formation shown, the underside of the existing footing cleaned and any loose material removed, the concrete placed to within 75mm of the footing and the gap dry packed with a 1:3 semi-dry mortar rammed tight after the concrete has cured for not less than 24 hours, with the bay inspected by the engineer or building control before the next is opened.",
    "Reinforced underpinning to have starter bars or couplers for the connection to the adjoining bays and to the basement slab, and the face to be cast against a shutter to receive the waterproofing. Where the existing footing is a corbelled brick footing or is found to be shallower or narrower than assumed, the engineer is to be informed before the bay is concreted. Underpinning to a party wall is to be carried out in accordance with the party wall award and the adjoining owner's surveyor is to be given the opportunity to inspect."]},
  {c:"Waterproofing (BS 8102)", t:"Waterproofing Design and Grade", p:[
    "The basement is to achieve Grade 3 to BS 8102:2022, being the grade for habitable accommodation in which no water penetration and no damp areas are acceptable and the internal environment is controlled. The standard describes three types of protection: Type A barrier protection by a membrane or coating, Type B structurally integral protection by a watertight concrete structure, and Type C drained protection by a cavity drainage membrane and pumped sump. For Grade 3 the standard recommends that where the water table is high or variable, or where the consequence of failure is serious, two of the three types are combined so that a defect in one is covered by the other, and that is the basis of the build-ups in Part A.",
    "The design is to be recorded in a waterproofing design statement identifying the grade, the types adopted, the assumed water table, the details at every junction, penetration and change of level, the pumping capacity and the maintenance regime, and is to be submitted to building control with the drawings. Every service penetration through the waterproofing is to be detailed with the manufacturer's puddle flange, sleeve or seal, and no penetration is to be made after the membrane is installed without the specialist's approval.",
    "NOTE — Type A alone on an existing masonry wall is the system most often found to have failed in basements that flood. A drained Type C system is forgiving of small defects because water that gets past the wall is collected and removed, which is why it is specified in every build-up here, as the second line of protection to a new wall and as the primary protection to a retained masonry wall."]},
  {c:"Waterproofing (BS 8102)", t:"Pumps, Sumps and Maintenance", p:[
    "Sump chambers and duplicate pumps as build-up BF1: the proprietary chamber recommended by the membrane manufacturer set into the slab at the low point of the perimeter channel with the channel laid to fall to it, the two pumps arranged to alternate and to run together at high level, with the battery back-up, the high-level alarm audible in the dwelling and, where the client requires it, a telemetry link. Discharge to rise to a point above the external ground and drain by gravity through a non-return valve to the surface water or combined drain; the pumps are not to discharge into the foul drain where a separate system exists.",
    "The system is to be commissioned by filling the sump and observing both pumps and the alarm, and the commissioning record, the manufacturer's maintenance schedule (normally an annual service with the channel jetted at every rodding point) and the location of the sump and rodding points are to be handed to the client. A service contract is recommended and the requirement for maintenance is to be recorded in the property information."]},
  {c:"Basement Walls", t:"Above-Ground Portions and Junctions", p:[
    "Where the basement wall projects above external ground level, the exposed portion is an external wall and is to be insulated to 0.18 W/m²K as a new wall or 0.30 as a retained wall, with a damp proof course at 150mm above ground linked to the waterproofing and a cavity tray or flashing at the junction with the wall above. The junction between the below-ground waterproofing and the above-ground damp proof course is a common point of failure and is to be detailed and inspected. Where the ground floor joists bear on the basement wall, the waterproofing is to be carried up behind the joist bearing and the joist ends protected."]},
  {c:"Additional Notes for Walls", t:"Damp Proof Courses, Ties, Movement and Attaching New to Existing", p:[
    "Damp proof courses in every wall rising above ground as the Above-Ground Portions and Junctions note, linked also to the floor membrane of the ground floor; stainless steel wall ties to BS EN 845-1 at 450mm vertical and 900mm horizontal centres (2.5 per m²) in any new cavity wall above ground, and at 300mm vertical centres within 225mm of every opening jamb and movement joint to PD 6697, with the cavity below the damp proof course filled with concrete to 225mm below it and weep holes above the fill; movement joints in new masonry at not more than 12m in clay brickwork and 6m in blockwork and where new work meets existing at a straight joint, with debonded ties and a compressible filler and sealant; new walls tied to existing with stainless steel starter profiles at 225mm centres or toothed in at alternate courses. Vapour control layers on the warm side of every insulated lining, continuous and sealed to the floor and ceiling."]},
  {c:"Basement Floors", t:"Floor Levels and Heave", p:[
    "The finished basement floor level and the ceiling height are to be set out from the engineer's structural depths and the waterproofing and insulation thicknesses in the build-ups, and confirmed on the sections before excavation; the Building Regulations set no minimum room height, but a habitable basement should have a clear ceiling height of not less than 2.3m for amenity, and the 2m headroom over the stair is a Part K requirement. Where a suspended or heave-protected slab is required on clay, the void former or the suspended construction is to the engineer's design and the cavity drain system is to be arranged so that the drained void is not the heave void."]},
  {c:"Internal Works", t:"Internal Doors, Lightwell Windows and Safety Glazing", p:[
    "Internal doors within the basement to be standard doorsets other than doors onto a protected stairway, which are to be FD30S fire doorsets (E 30 Sa, with intumescent strips and cold smoke seals) fitted with a self-closing device — this practice's standard, exceeding the FD20 minimum in Approved Document B Volume 1 for a protected stairway in a dwellinghouse; a door at the foot of the basement stair is not to open over the bottom step. Windows to lightwells to have a U-value not exceeding 1.4 W/m²K as the Conservation of Fuel & Power note, safety glazing to BS EN 12600 in the critical locations of Approved Document K (within 800mm of floor level in windows, 1500mm in doors and within 300mm of a door edge), and where relied on for escape the egress dimensions in the Means of Escape note."]},
  {c:"Lightwells & External Works", t:"Lightwells and External Stairs", p:[
    "Lightwells to be formed in reinforced concrete or engineering brick to the engineer's design with the waterproofing carried across the lightwell floor and up the walls, a drained floor to a trapped gully connected to the surface water drain with an anti-flood valve or a pumped connection where the drain is above the lightwell floor, and the lightwell floor set not less than 150mm below the sill of the basement window. Lightwell grilles at ground level to be removable from below for escape where the window is an escape window, to carry the pedestrian or vehicle load applicable, and to be fixed against unauthorised removal from above. Guarding of 1100mm to any open lightwell or external stair at ground level, not climbable and not passing a 100mm sphere.",
    "External steps to a basement to have rises of not more than 220mm and goings of not less than 220mm with a handrail, non-slip treads, and a drained landing at the bottom with a threshold of not less than 150mm to the door. Where the front lightwell is within the pavement zone, the highway authority's licence is required."]},
  {c:"Staircases & Headroom", t:"Stair to the Basement", p:[
    "New internal stair to the basement to Approved Document K for a private stair: pitch not more than 42 degrees, rise between 150mm and 220mm, going not less than 220mm, twice the rise plus the going between 550mm and 700mm, all rises and goings in a flight equal, headroom of 2m over the pitch line and at landings (the reduction to 1.9m at the centre and 1.8m at the side is permitted only for a loft conversion and does not apply here), a handrail at 900mm to 1000mm above the pitch line on at least one side and on both sides where the flight is 1m or wider with not less than 600mm clear between handrails, and guarding of 900mm to the open side and to any landing, not climbable by children, not passing a 100mm sphere and designed for the horizontal load in BS EN 1991-1-1 (0.36 kN/m for a dwelling). Tapered treads to have a going of not less than 50mm at the narrow end and to satisfy the going rule at the centre line (or 270mm from the narrow end where the stair is wider than 1m); landings at the top and bottom of the flight at least as long as the flight is wide and level, with no door swinging over a landing or the top step other than a cupboard door which leaves 400mm clear; open risers not to pass a 100mm sphere; timber stairs to BS 585 with the strings, treads and risers glued and wedged and the newels bolted to the floor structure. The stair is to be enclosed in fire-resisting construction where the means of escape strategy requires a protected stairway (see the Means of Escape note).",
    "Where the existing cellar stair is steep and is to be retained, it is to be brought to the standard above; an existing stair steeper than 42 degrees does not meet Part K for a habitable room and a relaxation is unlikely to be granted."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Means of Escape from a Basement Habitable Room", p:[
    "Because the single stair from a basement may be blocked by smoke from a fire in the basement or on the ground storey, Approved Document B Volume 1 requires that where a basement storey contains a habitable room one of the following is provided: an external door or window suitable for egress from the basement, being a window with an unobstructed openable area of not less than 0.33m² and not less than 450mm in both height and width with the bottom of the openable area not more than 1100mm above the floor (and not less than 800mm unless guarding to Approved Document K is provided), openable without a key from inside, opening into a lightwell from which a person can reach the ground; or a protected stairway leading from the basement to a final exit, enclosed in 30 minutes fire-resisting construction with FD30S fire doorsets (E 30 Sa, intumescent strips and cold smoke seals, self-closing) to every room opening onto it at basement and ground level, adopted as the firm's standard in place of the FD20 minimum.",
    "Where the egress route is a lightwell, the lightwell is to be large enough to stand in, not less than 1m from the window to the retaining wall, with a fixed ladder or steps to the ground where the depth exceeds 1.1m, and any grille above releasable from below without a key. Inner rooms in the basement are not to be bedrooms unless they have their own egress window."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Smoke Detection and Fire Resistance", p:[
    "Extend the dwelling's fire detection system to BS 5839-6 Grade D1 Category LD2 — mains powered, interlinked, with a tamper-proof standby supply — which exceeds the Grade D2 Category LD3 minimum in Approved Document B Volume 1 and is this practice's standard so that the whole house is protected, not only the basement, with a smoke alarm in the basement circulation space at the foot of the stair and in the basement habitable room, and a heat alarm in any basement kitchen or utility room, all interlinked with the alarms on the upper floors. The floor over the basement is to have 30 minutes fire resistance (REI 30) as the Floor Over Basement build-up, which is the period Table B2 of Approved Document B Volume 1 gives for a basement storey in a dwellinghouse where the lowest basement is not more than 10m deep. Where the house has more than one basement storey, the floor over the topmost basement is to have the higher of the period for the basement storey and the period for the ground or upper storey, as the footnotes to that table require. A dwellinghouse with a basement more than 10m deep falls outside the scope of the dwellinghouse guidance in Approved Document B Volume 1, and the fire strategy is then to be drawn from the guidance for buildings other than dwellings. Any wall separating the basement stair from the rooms is to be fire-resisting where the protected stairway option is adopted.",
    "Where the house will have four or more storeys including the basement, or a floor more than 4.5m above ground level, the stair from the basement to the upper floors is to be a protected stairway throughout or the alternative arrangements in Approved Document B Volume 1 Section 2 provided, and the fire strategy for the whole house is to be reviewed rather than the basement considered in isolation."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Smoke Alarms in Rented Dwellings, HMOs and Large Dwellings", p:[
    "Where the dwelling is or will be let, the Smoke and Carbon Monoxide Alarm (England) Regulations 2015 as amended in 2022 require at least one smoke alarm on every storey with living accommodation, which includes the basement, and a carbon monoxide alarm in every room with a fixed combustion appliance other than a gas cooker; the alarm system for a let house with a basement habitable room is to be not less than Grade D1 Category LD2 to BS 5839-6 with the landlord given the commissioning certificate. Where the house is or will be a house in multiple occupation, the local housing authority's HMO standards, the LACORS fire safety guidance and the licence conditions apply in addition: a basement bedroom in an HMO will normally need its own escape window or door and a Grade D1 Category LD2 or LD1 alarm system, and the intended occupation is to be confirmed in writing before the fire strategy is fixed. Where any storey exceeds 200m² the house is a large dwellinghouse for BS 5839-6 and a Grade A Category LD2 system with a control panel is recommended where it has three or more storeys."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Internal Linings and Cavity Barriers", p:[
    "Wall and ceiling linings in basement rooms to be Class C-s3, d2 or better, and Class B-s3, d2 in the circulation space at the foot of the stair and in the stair enclosure (Approved Document B Volume 1 Table 4.1); plasterboard satisfies both. The void behind the cavity drain membrane and the insulated lining is a concealed space and cavity barriers are to be provided at the junction with the floor over and around the stair enclosure where the concealed-spaces provisions of Approved Document B Volume 1 require them, using mineral wool that does not bridge the drainage path."]},
  {c:"Ventilation", t:"Ventilation of Basement Rooms — Approved Document F", p:[
    "Basement habitable rooms to be ventilated to Approved Document F Volume 1. Where the room has an openable window to a lightwell or to the open air, natural ventilation (System 1) may be used: purge ventilation by an openable area of not less than one twentieth of the floor area where the window opens more than 30 degrees, or one tenth where it opens between 15 and 30 degrees, and background ventilation of not less than 8000mm² equivalent area to every habitable room and kitchen and 4000mm² to every bathroom by trickle ventilators set not less than 1700mm above floor level, with the equivalent area marked or recorded and noise-attenuating ventilators used where the lightwell faces a road; the lightwell is to be at least as wide as the window height and open to the sky so that the window can ventilate the room. Internal doors undercut 10mm above the floor finish (7600mm² for a 760mm door). The openable areas are to be checked against the window schedule before issue.",
    "Where a basement room has no window, or the window opens into a lightwell too small to ventilate it, or the design air permeability is below 5 m³/(h·m²), the whole basement is to have a continuous mechanical system: continuous mechanical extract (System 3) with 4000mm² background ventilators to each habitable room, or mechanical ventilation with heat recovery (System 4) with no background ventilators, the supply terminals placed to avoid draughts and wet-room air not recirculated; continuous rates not less than 13 litres per second from a kitchen, 8 from each bathroom and utility room and 6 from a WC, the whole-dwelling rate not less than 0.3 litres per second per square metre of floor area (or the bedroom-count rate where greater), and 4 air changes per hour available for purge, ducted from and to the external air with the terminals clear of the lightwell floor and of any flue.",
    "Basement bathrooms, WCs and utility rooms served by intermittent extract to have fans to BS EN 13141-4 at 15, 6 and 30 litres per second respectively, and any basement kitchen 30 litres per second from a cooker hood over the hob (fitted 650mm to 750mm above it, to BS EN 13141-3) or 60 litres per second elsewhere, all ducted to the outside; fans may be wired to the light switch and are to have a 15 minute overrun where the room has no openable window. Ductwork rigid wherever possible with flexible duct limited to 1.5m at final connections, installed to BSRIA BG 43, insulated where it passes through unheated spaces and fire stopped or fitted with fire dampers where it passes through fire-resisting construction. Every fixed mechanical ventilation system to be commissioned with the flow rates measured to BS EN 13141-6 or 13141-7 and the results given to building control within five days of the final test and to the occupier in the Home User Guide. Open-flued combustion appliances are not to be installed in a basement, and any gas appliance is to be room-sealed with a carbon monoxide alarm in the room.",
    "NOTE — A basement with a cavity drainage system has a damp void behind the lining and a dry room in front of it; the room stays dry because the ventilation removes the moisture the occupants produce. Under-ventilating a basement produces condensation and mould on the cold corners within a season."]},
  {c:"Condensation (C2)", t:"Interstitial and Surface Condensation", p:[
    "A continuous vapour control layer is to be provided on the warm side of the insulated lining to every basement wall and to the floor, with all laps and penetrations sealed and the layer taped to the floor membrane and to the ceiling, so that moist internal air does not reach the cold masonry or concrete behind the insulation. Thermal bridging at the junction of the basement floor with the walls, at the stair, at the lightwell window and at the floor over is to be minimised by continuity of the insulation and by insulated cavity closers at the windows. A condensation risk analysis to BS EN ISO 13788 is to be provided for the wall lining where the specialist or building control requires it."]},
  {c:"Drainage & Pumping", t:"Drainage General", p:[
    "New underground drainage in 100mm PVC-U to BS EN 1401 or vitrified clay laid at not less than 1:40 on and surrounded by 100mm pea gravel with inspection chambers at every junction and change of direction and rodding eyes at the head of each run; above-ground pipework to BS EN 12056-2 with 75mm seal traps, 32mm branches to basins, 40mm to baths, showers and sinks and 100mm to WCs; the stack ventilated to the open air 900mm above any opening within 3m or fitted with an air admittance valve to BS EN 12380 provided at least one stack on the system remains open; pipes passing through the basement walls to be through puddle flanges or sealed sleeves designed by the waterproofing specialist and fire stopped where the wall is fire-resisting. Existing drains crossing the site of the basement are to be located by CCTV survey before excavation and diverted or supported to the engineer's design; drains shared with neighbouring houses in a terrace have been public sewers since 2011, and building over, under or within 3m of them, or diverting them, needs a build-over or diversion agreement with the sewerage undertaker with a CCTV survey before and after the works."]},
  {c:"Drainage & Pumping", t:"Foul Drainage from Below Sewer Level", p:[
    "Sanitary appliances in the basement will normally be below the level of the public sewer and are to discharge to a packaged pumping station or a macerator unit to BS EN 12050 sized for the appliances connected, with a non-return valve on the rising main, an alarm on pump failure, a sealed and ventilated chamber, and access for maintenance without entering the habitable room, discharging into the existing drain at a point above the sewer surcharge level. Gravity connections from a basement to a combined sewer are only permitted where the drain invert allows and an anti-flood valve to BS EN 13564 is fitted to protect the basement from sewer surcharge.",
    "Where a WC is connected to a macerator, a second WC discharging by gravity is to be available elsewhere in the dwelling. All new drainage to be tested by air or water before it is covered, in the presence of the Building Control Officer."]},
  {c:"Drainage & Pumping", t:"Surface Water and Lightwell Drainage", p:[
    "Lightwells, external stairs and any paved area falling towards the basement are to be drained to a trapped gully connected to the surface water drain with an anti-flood valve, or to the sump where a gravity connection is not available, with the gully grating below the level of the door threshold and the sill. Rainwater downpipes are not to discharge into the lightwell. The external ground is to fall away from the building and the existing surface water drainage inspected and repaired so that water is not directed to the basement walls; land drains at the base of the retaining wall, where the specialist specifies them, are to discharge to the surface water system or the sump."]},
  {c:"Services", t:"Electrical Installation", p:[
    "All electrical work to BS 7671 and Approved Document P by a registered competent person who is to issue an Electrical Installation Certificate and notify the work. The sump pumps and the mechanical ventilation are each to be on a dedicated circuit with RCD protection and the pump circuit identified at the consumer unit; the pump battery back-up is to be installed to the manufacturer's instructions and its condition indicated. Sockets and switches at 450mm to 1200mm above floor level, and no sockets within the lightwell zone or below the level of the perimeter drainage channel. Consumer units and meters are not to be relocated into the basement unless they are above the flood level of the sump alarm."]},
  {c:"Services", t:"Heating and Hot Water — Boilers, Heat Pumps, Underfloor Heating and Basement Siting", p:[
    "Extend the heating system with radiators fitted with thermostatic radiator valves or with underfloor heating in the screed of the new basement floor, sized for the heat loss of the basement rooms which, being largely below ground, is lower than an above-ground room of the same size. Boilers and hot water cylinders are not to be located in the basement unless they are room-sealed or unvented as appropriate, positioned above the level at which the sump high-level alarm sounds and provided with a condensate or discharge route that does not rely on gravity to the sewer without a pump. Gas pipework in the basement to be in ventilated ducts and the meter position to comply with the gas transporter's requirements.",
    "New and replacement wet heating systems are to be designed for a maximum flow temperature of 55°C with emitters sized accordingly. Gas boilers to have an ErP seasonal efficiency of not less than 92 per cent, a room-sealed flue with the terminal positioned to Approved Document J and the manufacturer's clearances and not less than 50mm from combustible material, inspection hatches of 300mm x 300mm at every joint and change of direction where a flue runs in a void, a carbon monoxide alarm to BS EN 50291 between 1m and 3m from the appliance, and, for a combination boiler, one of the additional efficiency measures in the Domestic Building Services Compliance Guide (flue gas heat recovery, weather or load compensation, or a smart thermostat with automation and optimisation). Controls to Approved Document L Volume 1 Section 5: time and temperature control of every heating zone, two zones where the dwelling exceeds 150m², thermostatic radiator valves, a cylinder thermostat and time control for hot water independent of the heating, and a boiler interlock; primary and accessible pipework, and all pipework outside the heated envelope, insulated to Table 4.4 of Approved Document L Volume 1. All gas work by a Gas Safe registered engineer, oil by OFTEC and solid fuel by HETAS.",
    "Heat pumps to MCS MIS 3005 with the heat loss calculation, emitter and cylinder sizing and the outdoor unit sited to the MCS 020 noise limit at the nearest neighbour's window; any back-up heater interlocked so it never leads. Oil-fired appliances installed by an OFTEC registered technician with the oil tank not less than 1.8m from the building, 760mm from the boundary, on a fire-resisting base extending 300mm beyond the tank, bunded to 110 per cent where there is a pollution risk, and fitted with a fire valve. Electric storage heaters with automatic charge control and panel heaters with time and temperature control in each room; electric underfloor heating on a dedicated circuit with 30mA RCD protection and a floor sensor thermostat to each zone, with continuity and insulation tests recorded before the floor is covered.",
    "Wet underfloor heating designed to BS EN 1264 with the floor surface temperature limited to 29°C (27 with resilient finishes), the pipes clipped to the floor insulation of the Basement Floors build-ups, 25mm edge insulation, a 65mm minimum screed over the pipes with movement joints at doorways, zone boundaries and bays over 40m² or 8m long, the system pressure tested before the screed is placed and commissioned to BS EN 1264-4 with the manifold zones labelled. Hot water storage to Approved Document G3: the cylinder capable of being stored at not less than 60°C to control legionella; unvented storage to BS EN 12897 installed by a person registered with a competent person scheme, with the temperature and pressure relief valve, expansion vessel and non-self-resetting thermal cut-out, a tundish within 500mm of the valve, the discharge pipe one size larger than the valve outlet routed as D1/D2 to a safe visible termination, and a warning label; hot water to baths limited to 48°C by a thermostatic mixing valve to BS EN 1111 or BS EN 1287. The commissioning certificate and the Benchmark record for every appliance to be given to building control and the occupier."]},
  {c:"Conservation of Fuel & Power", t:"Thermal Elements and U-values", p:[
    "New basement walls and floors are to achieve 0.18 W/m²K as new thermal elements and retained walls and floors 0.30 and 0.25 W/m²K respectively as retained elements, in accordance with Approved Document L Volume 1 Tables 4.2 and 4.3. The U-values of below-ground elements are to be calculated to BS EN ISO 13370 for a heated basement, taking account of the basement depth and the ground, which reduces the heat loss compared with the same construction above ground; the build-ups in Part A quote the below-ground values and identify the insulation needed. New windows to lightwells to have a U-value not exceeding 1.4 W/m²K and new external doors 1.4 W/m²K.",
    "Where the basement is a new extension below ground, the total area of windows, rooflights and doors in the extension is not to exceed 25 per cent of the extension floor area plus the area of any openings covered over, or the whole dwelling is to be shown to comply by a SAP calculation.",
    "NOTE — Approved Documents L1 and F1, 2026 editions, come into force on 24 March 2027. Work with a full plans application submitted before that date remains under the current standards provided work commences before 24 March 2028. Confirm the applicable values against the edition in force at the date of submission."]},
  {c:"Conservation of Fuel & Power", t:"Airtightness and Lighting", p:[
    "The vapour control layer and the plasterboard lining form the air barrier to the basement and are to be continuous and sealed at the floor, the ceiling, the stair and all penetrations. All new fixed internal and external lighting to have a luminous efficacy of not less than 75 lumens per circuit-watt (Approved Document L Volume 1, 2021 edition, Section 6), with LED luminaires or lamps throughout and fittings rated at less than 5 circuit-watts excluded from the count; recessed fittings in the basement ceiling to be fire-rated as the Floor Over Basement build-up and sealed to the vapour control layer. Lighting in the lightwell and on the external stair to be controlled by a photocell with a presence detector or time switch so that it is off in daylight and when not needed, with fittings of an IP rating suitable for the exposure."]},
 ]};
